\section{Common covariance structures}{

The covariance matrices \eqn{\Omega} and \eqn{\Sigma} are parameterized via
the lower-triangular Cholesky factors \eqn{O} and \eqn{L}.

For the \strong{random-effect covariance matrix \eqn{\Omega}}, the argument
\code{corr_re} controls whether correlations between random effects are included:
\itemize{
\item If \code{corr_re = FALSE}, \eqn{\Omega} is diagonal.
\item If \code{corr_re = TRUE}, \eqn{\Omega} is fully flexible.
}

For the \strong{error covariance matrix \eqn{\Sigma}}, \code{error_struc} selects
one of the following common structures:
\itemize{
\item \code{"full"} creates a flexible covariance matrix. The entry \code{L[2,2]} of the
Cholesky factor is fixed to one for scale identification, the first
Cholesky column is fixed to zero, and all remaining Cholesky entries are
free.
\item \code{"diag"} creates a diagonal covariance matrix. The first Cholesky diagonal
entry is fixed to one, and the remaining diagonal entries are free.
\item \code{"ident"} creates a covariance matrix proportional to the identity. All
Cholesky diagonal entries share one common free parameter.
\item \code{"fixed"} fixes the covariance matrix to the identity, i.e. all Cholesky
diagonal entries are fixed to one.
}
}

\section{Specifying the model formula}{

The structure of \code{formula} is \code{choice ~ A | B | C}, i.e., a standard
\code{\link[stats]{formula}} object but with (up to) three parts on the
right-hand side, separated by \code{|}, where
\itemize{
\item \code{choice} is the name of the discrete response variable,
\item \code{A} are names of \strong{alternative-specific covariates} with
\strong{a coefficient that is constant across alternatives},
\item \code{B} are names of \strong{covariates that are constant across
alternatives},
\item and \code{C} are names of \strong{alternative-specific covariates}
with \strong{alternative-specific coefficients}.
}

The following rules apply:
\enumerate{
\item By default, intercepts (referred to as alternative-specific
constants, ASCs) are added to the model. They can be removed by adding
\code{+ 0} in the second part, e.g., \code{choice ~ A | B + 0 | C}. To not include
any covariates of the second type but to estimate ASCs, add \code{1} in the
second part, e.g., \code{choice ~ A | 1 | C}. The expression
\code{choice ~ A | 0 | C} is interpreted as no covariates of the second type and
no ASCs.
\item To not include covariates of any type, add \code{0} in the respective
part, e.g., \code{choice ~ 0 | B | C}.
\item Some parts of the formula can be omitted when there is no ambiguity.
For example, \code{choice ~ A} is equivalent to \code{choice ~ A | 1 | 0}.
\item Multiple covariates in one part are separated by a \code{+} sign, e.g.,
\code{choice ~ A1 + A2}.
}
}

\examples{
# define model structure:
m <- mod$new(
  formula = choice ~ price | 1 | time,
  re = c("price", "ASC"),
  alt = 3,
  alt_names = c("train", "bus", "car"),
  ref_alt = "train",
  corr_re = TRUE,
  error_struc = "diag"
)
print(m)

# transform parameter vector to b, Omega, and Sigma:
theta <- runif(m$length_theta)
parameter <- m$build_parameter(theta)
parameter$b
parameter$Omega
parameter$Sigma

# reverse this operation
theta2 <- m$build_theta(
  b = parameter$b,
  Omega = parameter$Omega,
  Sigma = parameter$Sigma
)
all.equal(theta, theta2)

# is the model specification identified?
m$check_identifiability()
m$H_L <- diag(6)[, 4:6]; m$f_L[4, ] <- 0 # removed scale identification
m$check_identifiability()

}
